<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements: capabilities the app asks for, sealed into its code signature at build time.
  ≈ the <uses-permission> lines in AndroidManifest.xml. Like Android, anything not listed here
  is denied. Unlike Android, these are not shown to the user as a prompt; macOS just enforces them.

  The format is a "property list" (plist), Apple's XML key/value format. Each <key> is followed by
  its value: here <true/>, a boolean. project.yml points CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS at this file.

  Separate from these, some permissions are granted by the user in System Settings at runtime
  (Accessibility to press ⌘V, "Paste from Other Apps"). Those are asked for in code, not here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App Sandbox: the app runs in its own container, ~/Library/Containers/com.thattechedguy.Yank,
	  and can't read or write anything else on disk unless the user hands it a file (via a picker,
	  a drag, or the clipboard). ≈ Android's per-app private storage. Our database lives inside it.
	  We want it because it limits what a bug in Yank could touch, and the Mac App Store requires it.
	  macOS reads this key from the signature at launch and starts the process inside the container;
	  the system itself refuses any file access outside it, so our code never has to check.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Outgoing network connections. ≈ android.permission.INTERNET. Needed for link previews, which
	  fetch a copied page's title and icon; nothing else in Yank should ever use the network.
	  Without this key the sandbox refuses every outgoing connection, so the fetch would fail at
	  once. The key cannot narrow where the app connects, so local-network addresses must stay
	  blocked in code.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Read files the user picks in an Open dialog. Needed so the user can choose an app in a file
	  picker and Yank can read its bundle to add it to the ignored-apps list; the sandbox blocks that
	  read otherwise. macOS shows the Open dialog from outside the sandbox and hands the app only the
	  file the user picked. Read-only: Yank never writes outside its container.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
